# goal Decide what an unsupportable OpenAI parameter costs

This module exists because "not supported" is not one behaviour. The reasoning, and
the split between style and contract, is in
[_.hint#param_policy](../_.hint) — read that first; this file only records the
contract and how to extend it.

# invariant This module imports nothing {#no_imports}

`openai.js` imports it, so importing back would create a cycle for the sake of one
error class. `ParamReporter.report` therefore throws a plain `Error` with `.status`
and `.code` attached, which the HTTP layer already handles the same way.

If this file ever needs a helper, move the helper down here rather than importing up.

# invariant Every parameter is in exactly one bucket {#one_bucket}

`NATIVE`, `EMULATED`, `IGNORED`, `REFUSED`. A name in two buckets, or a REFUSED name
that some other module also handles, is a bug — the buckets are the whole policy and
they must be readable as one list.

Unknown names fall through to ignored **by design**, so the sets do not have to be
exhaustive to be correct.

# rule Adding support for a parameter is a bucket change, not an if {#adding_params}

When something becomes implementable, move its name from `REFUSED`/`IGNORED` into
`EMULATED` or `NATIVE` and implement it — do not special-case it upstream and leave
the name behind. A parameter honoured in `server.js` but still listed as ignored
reports a lie to the caller in `x_acp2api.ignored`.

# decision Caller tools have one policy shared by Chat and Responses {#tools_ignored}

`normalizeToolPolicy` validates definitions before any prompt turn. Only function
tools are accepted. Unknown/hosted/custom types, malformed nested definitions,
duplicate names and `strict: true` are 400 errors; an MCP `inputSchema` is not a
strict constrained-generation guarantee.

`tool_choice: auto` is supported. `none` disables discovery and rejects even a
stale cached call for that turn. `required` and a named function choice are 400
`unsupported_parameter` until the bridge can guarantee them. With
`server.tools: off`, non-empty caller definitions are dropped and reported as
`tools` in the same public annotation for Chat and Responses. The legacy
`functions` / `function_call` forms remain ignored.

Tool definitions are fixed for a conversation because MCP has no list-changed
channel here. Omission on a later request retains the cached list; an explicitly
different fingerprint is rejected before prompting.

Do not describe caller tools as the agent's statically configured `mcpServers`;
those are a separate, agent-owned tool surface.

The obligation that remains: tool calls and results already present in the history
must be rendered faithfully — see
[openai.js.hint#tool_history](./openai.js.hint). Dropping the definitions is a
choice; garbling the record of what was already done is a bug.

`response_format` stays refused: nothing gives the caller its guarantee back.

# func classify {#classify}

## arg body
The raw request body. Only its **keys** matter, except for `n`, where `1` and `null`
are indistinguishable from absent and so are not worth a 400.

## result
`{ignored: string[], refused: [{key, why}]}` — `ignored` sorted, so the value echoed
back to callers and asserted in tests is stable.

# func normalizeToolPolicy {#tool_policy}

## arg body
The raw Chat or Responses request containing `tools` and `tool_choice`.

## arg shape
`chat` for nested function definitions; `responses` for flat definitions.

## result
`{choice, tools, toolsProvided}`. `choice` is `auto` or `none`; `tools` is empty
under `none`; `toolsProvided` distinguishes omission from an explicit list.

It throws a plain error with status 400 and code `unsupported_parameter` for
`required`, named choice, unsupported types, and `strict: true`; malformed choices
and definitions use code `invalid_request_error`.

# func ignoredNestedKeys {#nested_keys}

## arg value
The already type-validated object parameter, or null.

## arg prefix
The public top-level parameter name used to form dotted annotation paths.

## arg supported
A Set of nested keys actually honoured by the caller.

## result
A sorted list of unsupported dotted paths. Accepting a nested field is never silent;
callers merge these names into the ordinary ignored-parameter annotation.

# invariant ParamReporter is stable and bounded {#report}

`ParamReporter.report(model, ignored)` returns the same names for the public
annotation. In `error` mode it throws status 400 `unsupported_parameter`; in
`ignore` mode it is silent. Warning mode logs one line per `(model, parameter)` for
the process lifetime, not per request. A client looping with `temperature` would
otherwise produce one line per call forever.

# test params

- style parameters (`temperature`, `top_p`, `seed`) are ignored, never refused
- `tools`, `tool_choice: auto|none`, and Chat `reasoning_effort` are emulated, not ignored
- `required`, named choice, `strict: true`, unsupported types, malformed nested
  definitions and duplicate names are rejected before a prompt turn
- valid nested JSON Schema under function `parameters` is preserved unchanged
- old `functions` / `function_call` remain ignored
- what remains contract-changing (`response_format`, `audio`, `modalities`) is still refused
- `n: 1` and `n: null` pass; `n: 3` is refused
- an unknown future field is ignored rather than refused
- unknown fields inside supported objects are reported as stable dotted paths
- native and emulated names appear in neither list
- the reporter logs each (model, parameter) pair exactly once across repeated calls
- mode `ignore` logs nothing; mode `error` throws with status 400 and the right code
